<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="notes-week-02">
  <title>Week 2</title>

  <subsection>
    <title>Chapter 2</title>
    <p>
      <alert>Reading Quiz 2 Covers 2.1 through 2.6</alert>
    </p>
    <p>
      <alert>Reading Quiz 3 Covers 2.9</alert>
    </p>

    <p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            Sections 2.1 - 2.3 are setting the context for Newton's laws. Important things to note here:
            they are very, very useful, but have limitations. The textbook isn't a history book, but note
            that it took a great deal of work to distill Newton's laws in the form we use today.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Section 2.4 is the modern interpretation of Newton's first law. A very common mistake is to 
            try and apply the 2nd law while implicitly using an accelerated frame of reference. What
            defines an inertial frame? Difficult question. For now, we'll accept that the reference frame
            fixed to the surface of the Earth is approximately inertial, and go from there.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Sections 2.5 and 2.6 describe Newton's 2nd and 3rd laws in more detail. Note that the 3rd law
            relates pairs of forces acting on different objects. The 2nd law will be our workhorse for
            the term: given an object, we can identify the external forces acting on it, and write
            the 2nd law for its motion.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Section 2.8  is actually much more important than you might think. First, checking that your answers 
            <q>have the right units</q> is one of the most basic sanity checks, and will need to become second nature to you 
            in every single problem you solve (and not only in this class but throughout physics!). 
            Second, and more subtly, dimension-based arguments can often tell you surprisingly much about physical 
            phenomena without even doing any calculations. There is some additional reading about this on Canvas, totally
            optional.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Section 2.9 introduces an algorithm to applying Newton's laws. I am going to ask you to follow this algorithm
            <alert>to the letter</alert> in the homework. You won't need to number all the steps, but we want to see it.
            It might feel unnatural to be this systematic if you <q>know how to solve a problem intuitively</q>, but what
            do we do when intuition fails? We look for explicit strategies. Trying to go directly from problem statement
            to solution is a common hurdle from students in this course. Here's a secret: most of the time, no one
            can do that. Take your time, be patient and systematic. That will develop your intuition and then, after
            solving many problems, things will come faster.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            More on section 2.9: <term>constraint equations</term> are likely to be new to you, especially this way of
            finding a constraint on the positions of the objects and taking two derivatives to obtain an acceleration 
            constraint. Practice doing this for every single problem that has a constraint. In order for your constraint
            work well with Newton's laws, the coordinates you choose need to be attached to an inertial frame. To get
            constraints right, it will be critical to have a clear diagram with all coordinate axes clearly defined, so
            you understand how to convert, for instance, a length <m>L</m> to some difference in coordinates
            <m>x_2-x_1</m>, for instance.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </subsection>

</section>
